```go
package middleware

import "net/http"

// SetHeaders is a convenience handler to set response headers from an
// http.Header. The supplied headers are snapshotted when SetHeaders is called:
// names are canonicalized and the map and every value slice are copied. On
// each request the configured headers replace any response headers with the
// same name (unlisted headers are left untouched) before the next handler
// runs, and the response receives its own copy of the values so later
// mutation cannot affect subsequent requests.
func SetHeaders(headers http.Header) func(http.Handler) http.Handler {
	if len(headers) == 0 {
		return func(next http.Handler) http.Handler {
			return next
		}
	}

	cfg := make(http.Header, len(headers))
	for name, values := range headers {
		cfg[http.CanonicalHeaderKey(name)] = append([]string(nil), values...)
	}

	return func(next http.Handler) http.Handler {
		return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
			h := w.Header()
			for name, values := range cfg {
				h[name] = append([]string(nil), values...)
			}
			next.ServeHTTP(w, r)
		})
	}
}
```

Design decisions already made, follow them literally:
- `len(headers) == 0` is the nil/empty no-op path (identity middleware returning `next`).
- Snapshot at factory creation: `cfg` deep-copies the map and each value slice; keys are canonicalized with `http.CanonicalHeaderKey`.
- Per request: direct map assignment `h[name] = ...` replaces the canonical-named response header and leaves unlisted headers untouched; `append([]string(nil), values...)` gives each response a fresh value slice so a downstream handler mutating that slice cannot alter `cfg` used by later requests. No merge logic is needed because callers guarantee inputs have no duplicate names under case folding.

